# Unvibe Hackathons

Hackathons should be awesome. So why do so many suck?

## Introduction

While I was a DevRel for the Protocol Labs ecosystem I judged over 1,000 hacakthon projects in a single year. Since then, I've entered countless blockchain and AI hackathons, mainly as a solo dev.

The plain truth: the spirit of hackathons is collaboration, innovation, and community building. The implementation often fails participants on all sides creating friction, bottlenecks, confusion and disappointment from builders, and exhausted judges who wish they had the time to do things differently.

## LLM-as-a-Judge

This project explores using the [LLM-as-a-Judge](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LLM-as-a-Judge) NLP conceptual framework in combination with blockchain/crypto primitives to relieve painpoints in hackathon participation.

Initially, I set out to create a judge for each track this hacakthon sponsors. The scope for this submission was narrowed to demonstrate the LLM-as-a-judge pattern end-to-end through a single sponsor judge (0G), with the supporting infrastructure (safe-repo, event-policy, orchestrator) and conventions (per-sponsor SKILL.md, calibration discipline, on-disk findings contract) designed to scale to additional sponsors.

## Calibration Process

Something interesting happened when I did what a lot of us do when asked to evaluate hackathon projects: I underestimated the depth and quality of integration based off a quick sweep of the repo in all three example projects I evaluated. I selected some random projects from the ETH Global showcase that feature 0G Labs and evaluated them manually for what I thought the findings of a deterministic filtering of the project would find. I was wrong all three times in ways that would penalize the builder team who submitted these projects if I were doing an actual judging. The simple fact is, without deep product knowledge, it can be difficult to accurately gauge these projects with the quick repo sweep a lot of judges are used to doing. A deterministic filtering can result in a focused list that human judges can evaluate against "How well does this fit the track?" rather than "Is this a good project for this track at all." That distinction can mean all the difference for a team that builds a solid project but lacks experience creating a proper hackathon README.
